 would translate  well to animation and you could tell  that the creators of the the book really  love Japanese pop culture in fact the  whole thing felt like a love letter to  Japan I mean there were references to  miyazaki and you know stuff that only  like true nerds would know you know like  me and and it just had a cool feel to it  and but at the core of it we saw that  there could be a really emotional story  between this 14 year old super genius  who loses his older brother and this  robot who essentially  comes his healer and his surrogate big  brother and and so I pitched big hero 6  along with about five or six other  Marvel titles to to John and he  immediately gravitated towards this one  and it was because of the emotional  story you know I think he would just it  really spoke to him and so he on the  spot green let it and said okay get  going didn't you didn't intentionally  dampen down the other ones you know I'll  be perfectly honest bill I myself and  some of the other artists had done  actually quite a bit of artwork for a  lot of the other projects this is the  one we didn't do any artwork for it was  the smallest if you think of our story  rooms the the boards lined to the room  so all of the pitches lined the room and  and they got progressively smaller  because as you know with less artwork  and this is the one that had no artwork  just a cover like I think I had maybe  the pictures of the covers but that  spark you could feel it right away it  was that emotional story motional core  was there he lit up in that that was  what do you know immediately he was like  that's the one and so we called a  meeting with Marvel and said John picked  big hero 6 and into marvel I mean it was  one of them you know this is one of the  lesser-known properties that they had  done and look it up and so they you know  we called a meeting with all their guys  and and joe quesada who is their chief  creative officer he sort of the John  Lasseter of Marvel I guess an